# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
EN = {}
EN[215] = "<<EMPRESS>>“Rise!”<<N>> a commanding and at the same time pleasant voice sounded above them. Some of the courtiers bustled and nudged the Zaporozhians."
EN[216] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“We will not rise, Mother! We will not rise! We will die, but we will not rise!”<<N>> cried the Zaporozhians."
EN[217] = "Potemkin bit his lips; at last he came up himself and whispered a command to one of the Zaporozhians. The Zaporozhians rose."
EN[218] = "Then the blacksmith too dared to raise his head and saw standing before him a woman of small height, even somewhat stout, powdered, with blue eyes and at the same time a majestically smiling air which knew how to subdue everyone and could belong only to a reigning woman."
EN[219] = "<<EMPRESS>>“His Serene Highness promised to acquaint me today with my people, whom I have never yet seen,”<<N>> said the lady with the blue eyes, examining the Zaporozhians with curiosity. <<EMPRESS>>“Are you well kept here?”<<N>> she went on, coming nearer."
EN[220] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Thank you, Mother! They give good provisions, though the sheep here are quite unlike ours in Zaporozhia; but why not live somehow?...”"
EN[221] = "Potemkin frowned, seeing that the Zaporozhians were saying quite the opposite of what he had taught them..."
EN[222] = "One of the Zaporozhians, drawing himself up, stepped forward: <<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Have mercy, Mother! How has Thy faithful people angered Thee? Did we hold the hand of the filthy Tatar? Did we agree in anything with the Turk? Have we betrayed Thee in deed or in thought? Why then this displeasure? Before, we heard that Thou orderest fortresses to be built everywhere against us; later we heard that Thou wishest to turn us into carabineers; now we hear of new misfortunes. What is the fault of the Zaporozhian Host? That it took Thy army across Perekop and helped Thy generals cut down the Crimeans?...”"
EN[223] = "Potemkin was silent and carelessly cleaned with a small brush the diamonds with which his hands were studded."
EN[224] = "<<EMPRESS>>“What is it that you want?”<<N>> Catherine asked with concern."
EN[225] = "The Zaporozhians looked meaningfully at each other."
EN[226] = "<<VAKULA>>“Now is the time! The Empress asks what you want!”<<N>> the blacksmith said to himself and suddenly threw himself on the ground:"
EN[227] = "<<VAKULA>>“Your Imperial Majesty, do not order me executed, order me pardoned! What, no offence meant to Your Imperial mercy, are the little shoes on Your feet made of? I think no cobbler in any kingdom in the world could make them so. My God, what if my wife wore such shoes!”"
EN[228] = "The Empress laughed. The courtiers laughed too. Potemkin both frowned and smiled. The Zaporozhians began to nudge the blacksmith's arm, wondering whether he had gone mad."
EN[229] = "<<EMPRESS>>“Rise!”<<N>> said the Empress kindly. <<EMPRESS>>“If you want so much to have such shoes, it is not hard to do. Bring him at once the dearest shoes, with gold! Truly, I like this simplicity very much! Here,”<<N>> the Empress went on, fixing her eyes on a middle-aged man standing apart from the others, with a full but somewhat pale face, whose modest coat with large mother-of-pearl buttons showed that he did not belong to the courtiers, <<EMPRESS>>“is a subject worthy of your witty pen!”"
EN[230] = "<<WRITER>>“Your Imperial Majesty is too gracious. For this one would need at least a La Fontaine!”<<N>> answered the man with the pearl buttons, bowing."
EN[231] = "<<EMPRESS>>“Upon my honour I tell you: I am still beside myself over your 'Brigadier'. You read wonderfully well! But,”<<N>> the Empress went on, turning again to the Zaporozhians, <<EMPRESS>>“I have heard that in the Sich you never marry.”"
EN[232] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Why, Mother! Surely a man, as You Yourself know, cannot live without a wife,”<<N>> answered the same Zaporozhian who had talked with the blacksmith, and the blacksmith was surprised to hear that this Zaporozhian, who knew the learned language so well, was speaking to the Empress, as if on purpose, in the coarsest dialect, usually called peasant speech. <<VAKULA>>“A cunning people!”<<N>> he thought to himself. <<VAKULA>>“Surely he does this for a reason.”"
EN[233] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“We are not monks,”<<N>> the Zaporozhian went on, <<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“but sinful men. We are as prone, like all honest Christendom, to forbidden food. There are not a few among us who have wives, only they do not live with them in the Sich. There are some who have wives in Poland; there are some who have wives in Ukraine; there are some who have wives in Turkey too.”"
EN[234] = "At this time the shoes were brought to the blacksmith. <<VAKULA>>“My God, what an ornament!”<<N>> he cried joyfully, seizing the shoes. <<VAKULA>>“Your Imperial Majesty! If shoes like these are on Your feet, and in them, most likely, Your Honour goes skating on the ice, what must the feet themselves be? I think, at the very least, of pure sugar.”"
EN[235] = "The Empress, who truly had the most shapely and charming feet, could not help smiling at hearing such a compliment from the lips of the simple blacksmith, who in his Zaporozhian dress might have passed for a handsome man, in spite of his dark face."
EN[236] = "Delighted by such gracious attention, the blacksmith was about to question the Empress closely about everything: whether it is true that tsars eat nothing but honey and lard, and the like; but feeling the Zaporozhians nudge him in the ribs, he decided to be silent; and when the Empress, turning to the old men, began to ask how they lived in the Sich and what customs they had, he stepped back, bent to his pocket, said quietly: <<VAKULA>>“Carry me out of here quickly!”<<N>> and suddenly found himself beyond the barrier."
EN[238] = "<<WEAVER'S WIFE>>“Drowned! By God, drowned! May I not move from this spot if he did not drown!”<<N>> babbled the stout weaver's wife, standing in a knot of Dikanka women in the middle of the street."
EN[239] = "<<WOMAN>>“What, am I some kind of liar? Have I stolen someone's cow? Have I put the evil eye on someone, that I am not believed?”<<N>> cried a woman in a Cossack coat with a violet nose, waving her hands. <<WOMAN>>“May I never want to drink water again if old Pereperchikha did not see with her own eyes how the blacksmith hanged himself!”"
EN[240] = "<<HEAD>>“The blacksmith hanged himself? Well, there you are!”<<N>> said the Headman, who was coming out from Chub's; he stopped and pushed his way closer to those who were talking."
EN[241] = "<<WEAVER'S WIFE>>“Say rather may you never want to drink vodka, you old drunkard!”<<N>> answered the weaver's wife. <<WEAVER'S WIFE>>“One would have to be as mad as you to hang oneself! He drowned! Drowned in an ice-hole! I know that as surely as that you were at the tavern-keeper's just now.”"
EN[242] = "<<WOMAN>>“Shameless creature! Look what she is reproaching me with!”<<N>> retorted the woman with the violet nose angrily. <<WOMAN>>“Be quiet, you worthless thing! Don't I know that the deacon comes to you every evening?”"
EN[243] = "The weaver's wife flared up."
EN[244] = "<<WEAVER'S WIFE>>“What deacon? Who is the deacon coming to? What are you lying about?”"
EN[245] = "<<DEACON'S WIFE>>“The deacon?”<<N>> sang out the deacon's wife, pressing toward the disputants, in a hareskin coat covered with blue Chinese cotton. <<DEACON'S WIFE>>“I will give you a deacon! Who is saying deacon?”"
EN[246] = "<<WOMAN>>“There, that is who the deacon visits!”<<N>> said the woman with the violet nose, pointing to the weaver's wife."
EN[247] = "<<DEACON'S WIFE>>“So it is you, bitch,”<<N>> said the deacon's wife, advancing on the weaver's wife, <<DEACON'S WIFE>>“so it is you, witch, who cast a fog on him and give him unclean potions to drink, so that he will come to you!”"
EN[248] = "<<WEAVER'S WIFE>>“Leave me alone, Satan!”<<N>> said the weaver's wife, backing away."
EN[249] = "<<DEACON'S WIFE>>“Look at her, the accursed witch, may you never live to see your children, you worthless creature! Phew!”<<N>> Here the deacon's wife spat right in the weaver's wife's eyes."
EN[250] = "The weaver's wife wanted to do the same, but instead spat into the unshaven beard of the Headman, who, the better to hear everything, had worked his way up to the very disputants. <<HEAD>>“Ah, foul woman!”<<N>> cried the Headman, wiping his face with the skirt of his coat and raising his whip. This movement made everyone scatter, swearing, in different directions. <<HEAD>>“What filth!”<<N>> he kept repeating, still wiping himself. <<HEAD>>“So the blacksmith has drowned! My God, and what a fine painter he was! What strong knives, sickles and ploughs he knew how to forge! What strength he had! Yes,”<<N>> he went on, thinking, <<HEAD>>“there are few such men in our village. That is why, while still sitting in the accursed sack, I noticed that the poor fellow was in a very bad mood. So much for the blacksmith! He was, and now he is no more! And I was just about to have my dappled mare shod!...”<<N>> And, full of such Christian thoughts, the Headman slowly trudged to his cottage."
EN[251] = "Oksana was confused when such news reached her. She put little faith in Pereperchikha's eyes and the women's talk: she knew that the blacksmith was pious enough not to decide to destroy his soul. But what if he really had gone away with the intention of never returning to the village? And it is doubtful whether another such fine fellow as the blacksmith could be found anywhere! And he had loved her so! He had endured her whims longer than anyone! All night, under her blanket, the beauty turned from her right side to her left, from her left to her right, and could not sleep. Now, tossing in a bewitching nakedness which the darkness of night hid even from herself, she almost aloud scolded herself; now, growing quiet, she resolved to think of nothing, and kept thinking. And she was all aflame; and by morning she was head over heels in love with the blacksmith."
EN[252] = "Chub showed neither joy nor sorrow at Vakula's fate. His thoughts were occupied with one thing: he could not forget Solokha's treachery, and even asleep he did not cease to curse her."
EN[253] = "Morning came. The whole church was full of people even before daylight. Elderly women in white head-cloths, in white cloth coats, crossed themselves piously at the very entrance of the church. Gentlewomen in green and yellow jackets, and some even in blue kuntushes with gold whiskers behind, stood in front of them. Girls with a whole shop of ribbons wound on their heads, and on their necks strings of beads, crosses and ducats, tried to push in still nearer to the iconostasis. But in front of all stood the gentlemen and plain peasants with moustaches, with forelocks, with thick necks and chins just shaved, mostly in cloaks, from under which a white, or on some a blue, coat was showing. On all faces, wherever you looked, the holiday could be seen: the Headman licked his lips, imagining how he would break his fast with sausage; the girls were thinking how they would skate on the ice with the lads; the old women whispered their prayers more zealously than ever. All over the church one could hear how the Cossack Svyerbyhuz made his bows. Only Oksana stood as if not herself. She prayed and did not pray. In her heart so many different feelings were crowded, one more vexing than another, one sadder than another, that her face expressed only strong confusion; tears trembled in her eyes. The girls could not understand the cause and did not suspect that the blacksmith was to blame. Yet Oksana was not the only one occupied with the blacksmith. All the villagers noticed that the holiday was somehow not a holiday; that something was somehow missing. As bad luck would have it, the deacon, after his journey in the sack, was hoarse and quavered in a barely audible voice; true, the visiting singer took the bass splendidly, but how much better if the blacksmith had been there, who always, as soon as they sang Our Father or Who the Cherubim, used to go up to the choir and lead from there in the same tune as they sing in Poltava. Besides, he alone performed the duties of church warden. Already morning service was over; after morning service, the liturgy was over... where, indeed, had the blacksmith got to?"
EN[255] = "Still faster through the rest of the night the devil sped back with the blacksmith. And in a moment Vakula found himself beside his cottage. At that moment a cock crowed. <<VAKULA>>“Where to?”<<N>> he cried, seizing by the tail the devil who wanted to run away. <<VAKULA>>“Wait, my friend, not everything yet: I have not yet thanked you.”<<N>> Then, seizing a switch, he gave him three blows, and the poor devil took to his heels like a peasant who has just been flogged by the assessor. So, instead of leading astray, tempting and making fools of others, the enemy of mankind was himself made a fool of. After this Vakula went into the porch, burrowed into the hay and slept till noon. Waking, he was frightened when he saw that the sun was already high: <<VAKULA>>“I have slept through morning service and the liturgy!”<<N>> The pious blacksmith sank into dejection, reasoning that surely God had purposely, as a punishment for his sinful intention of destroying his soul, sent him a sleep that did not even let him be in church on such a solemn holiday. But, comforting himself that next week he would confess this to the priest, and from today would begin to make fifty prostrations every day for a whole year, he looked into the cottage; but no one was in it. Evidently Solokha had not yet returned. Carefully he took the shoes from his bosom and again marvelled at the costly work and the wonderful adventure of the past night; he washed, dressed as well as he could, put on the very clothes which he had got from the Zaporozhians, took from the chest a new cap of Reshetilovka lambskin with a blue top, which he had not worn once since buying it back in Poltava; he also took a new belt of all colours; he put all this together with a whip in a kerchief and set off straight for Chub's."
EN[256] = "Chub goggled when the blacksmith came in to him, and did not know what to wonder at: whether that the blacksmith had risen from the dead, or that the blacksmith dared to come to him, or that he had dressed up as such a dandy and a Zaporozhian. But he was still more astonished when Vakula untied the kerchief and laid before him a brand-new cap and a belt such as had never been seen in the village, and himself fell at his feet and said in a pleading voice: <<VAKULA>>“Have mercy, father! Do not be angry! Here is a whip for you: beat me as much as your heart desires, I give myself up; I repent of everything; beat me, only do not be angry! You once were brothers in arms with my late father, ate bread and salt together and drank to the bargain.”"
EN[257] = "Not without secret pleasure Chub saw the blacksmith, who feared no one in the village and bent coins and horseshoes in his hand like buckwheat pancakes, lying at his feet. So as not to lower himself further, Chub took the whip and struck him three times on the back. <<CHUB>>“Well, that is enough for you, get up! Always listen to old people! Let us forget all that was between us! Now say, what do you want?”"
EN[258] = "<<VAKULA>>“Give Oksana to me, father!”"
EN[259] = "Chub thought a little, looked at the cap and belt, the cap was a wonder, the belt was not inferior to it, remembered treacherous Solokha, and said resolutely: <<CHUB>>“Good! Send the matchmakers!”"
EN[260] = "<<OKSANA>>“Ah!”<<N>> cried Oksana, stepping over the threshold and seeing the blacksmith, and fixed her eyes on him with amazement and joy."
EN[261] = "<<VAKULA>>“Look what shoes I have brought you!”<<N>> said Vakula. <<VAKULA>>“The very ones the Empress wears.”"
EN[262] = "<<OKSANA>>“No! No! I don't need shoes!”<<N>> she said, waving her hands and not taking her eyes from him. <<OKSANA>>“I will, even without shoes...”<<N>> She did not finish and blushed."
EN[263] = "The blacksmith came nearer and took her hand; the beauty lowered her eyes. Never before had she been so wonderfully pretty. The delighted blacksmith kissed her softly, and her face flamed more and she became lovelier still."
EN[265] = "A bishop of blessed memory passed through Dikanka, praised the place where the village stands, and, driving along the street, stopped before a new cottage. <<BISHOP>>“And whose is this painted cottage?”<<N>> the bishop asked a handsome woman with a child in her arms, who was standing near the door. <<OKSANA>>“The blacksmith Vakula's!”<<N>> Oksana said to him, bowing, because it was she. <<BISHOP>>“Splendid! Splendid work!”<<N>> said the bishop, examining the doors and windows. And the windows were all outlined round with red paint; and on the doors everywhere were Cossacks on horses with pipes in their teeth. But the bishop praised Vakula still more when he learned that he had served the church penance and had painted the whole left choir for nothing, with green paint and red flowers. And that is not all: on the wall at the side, as you enter the church, Vakula painted the devil in hell, so ugly that everyone spat when they passed by; and the women, as soon as a child began to cry in their arms, would bring it to the picture and say: <<WOMAN>>“Look, see what an ugly thing is painted!”<<N>> and the child, holding back its little tears, squinted at the picture and pressed to its mother's breast."
